# Defect 097 over an array of handles the program owns: four lives begun by
# `acquires ob_free`, each written into its own element at a computed index,
# and each ended by giving that element to `ob_free`.
#
# The counter is the witness (spec § 13: a handle nobody consumes aborts when
# `main` returns, saying how many), and both of its failures were run on
# 2026-09-25 by editing this file: writing `f.a[i / 2 * 2]`, so one slot keeps
# its `nullptr` and one handle is written over, is 134 when `ob_free` reads the
# null; releasing three of the four is 134 with `1 C handle(s) never given
# back`. So exit 0 says all four writes landed where the four releases found
# them. The write itself adds no handle-set traffic: the life begins at the
# call, and the store is an assignment where the element lies.
#
# **It counts the releases in C since the same evening.** Its first form read
# the record back after giving all four handles to `ob_free`, `released: 0`:
# C reading four freed objects, which the static pool hid. The poison of panel
# 177's item 1 refuses that crossing before C, as it should, so the count is
# `ob_freed`, which takes no handle.

extern "fixedbugs-an-acquired-handle-is-written-into-a-fixed-array.h"
    record Ob tag ob
    record Four tag four
        a: Ob[4]
    function ob_new(n: i64) -> Ob acquires ob_free
    function ob_free(o: Ob consumes)
    function four_sum(f: Four) -> i64
    function ob_freed() -> i64

function main()
    f: Four @ Four(a: [nullptr, nullptr, nullptr, nullptr])
    n: i64 @ 1

    for i in range(from: 0, to: 4)
        f.a[i] @ ob_new(n: n)
        n @ n * 10

    print("sum: ", four_sum(f: f))

    for i in range(from: 0, to: 4)
        ob_free(o: f.a[i])

    print("released: ", ob_freed())
